\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces The pipeline chain made concrete: real $|U|^2$ intensity at the corneal plane (left column, step~1 of the chain above, post-pinhole/pre-lens) versus the real retinal intensity after the full lens-phase and myopic-axial-distance propagation (right column, step~5). Top row $M=0$D, $a_{\mathrm {hole}}=2000\,\mu $m; bottom row $M=8$D, $a_{\mathrm {hole}}=400\,\mu $m -- the two extremes of the real per-$M$ aperture map (\texttt {array\_data/evaluation\_results/} \texttt {PRIMARY\_WINNER\_PER\_M.csv}, mtime 2026-09-13 14:10). The corneal-plane panels show genuine near-field Fresnel ringing at the aperture edge -- the field has propagated only $d_{\mathrm {gap}}=12$\,mm and still closely resembles the aperture's own geometric shape; the retinal panels show the same energy after the myopic axial propagation, now dominated by defocus blur rather than the aperture's edge structure. Both column pairs generated by the same \texttt {run\_pipeline\_point()} call (one call yields both fields; no extra propagation was run to make this comparison) -- \texttt {figure\_scripts/build\_corneal\_illumination.py} (this paper, new) and \texttt {Array\_Design/array\_geometry\_lab/retinal\_light\_distribution\_simulation/} \texttt {realistic\_heatmaps/build\_realistic\_heatmaps.py} (mtime of its outputs: 2026-09-28 11:15--11:16). Each panel normalized to its own peak -- absolute brightness is not comparable across panels without a source-power calibration this project has not made (Section~\ref {sec:limitations-optics}).}}{16}{figure.2.1}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Real retinal-plane intensity ($I_{\mathrm {retina}}=|U_{\mathrm {retina}}|^2$, straight-ahead gaze) for all eight modeled myopia powers, left to right: $M=0,1,2,3$D (top row), $M=4,5,6,8$D (bottom row), each at that $M$'s real \texttt {PRIMARY\_WINNER\_PER\_M.csv} aperture. Every panel is the actual ASM wave-optics output, not a schematic -- the growing blur radius from $M{=}0$ to $M{=}8$ visible here is the same physical quantity the LogMAR curve above is predicting a number for. Source: \texttt {Array\_Design/array\_geometry\_lab/retinal\_light\_distribution\_simulation/} \texttt {realistic\_heatmaps/realistic\_heatmap\_M\{0,1,2,3,4,5,6,8\}.png}, all eight generated 2026-09-28 11:15--11:16 (verified via \texttt {stat}); each panel normalized to its own peak, not comparable in absolute brightness across $M$ (Section~\ref {sec:limitations-optics}).}}{22}{figure.3.1}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Mean BRISQUE score per condition, generated directly from the same CSV the table above is drawn from (\texttt {ExperimentalComparison/ExP1\_clarity\_analysis/} \texttt {measurements/brisque\_scores.csv}, mtime 2026-09-09) -- table and figure are guaranteed consistent by construction, both computed from this one file, not cross-checked after the fact.}}{25}{figure.4.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Measured spot radius vs.\ screen displacement, all seven conditions, from the same CSV the growth-slope table above is fit from (\texttt {ExperimentalComparison/ExP3\_frosted\_screen/measurements/} \texttt {all\_spot\_measurements.csv}, mtime 2026-09-02). The no-pinhole control's much steeper growth is directly visible, not just implied by the slope ratio in the table; the pinhole conditions' near-overlapping, shallow curves make the non-monotonicity in 600\,$\mu $m's slope (lowest of the six, not the largest or smallest aperture) a real, visually confirmable feature of the raw data rather than a fitting artifact.}}{26}{figure.4.2}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Independent validation of the ghost-separation formula $\Delta r(s,M)=sM/(P_0-M)$ against measured (incoherent) two-hole separation from a direct wave-optics simulation ($\mathtt {grid\_N}=4096$, $a_{\mathrm {hole}}=400\,\mu $m, $d_{\mathrm {gap}}=12$\,mm), sweeping pitch $s$ at 8 myopia powers ($M=0$--$6$D). Right panel: $R^2=0.9951$ for the corrected formula, versus $R^2=-1701.7$ for the legacy formula this project explicitly disproved and stopped using (a negative $R^2$ -- worse than predicting the mean). A free 2-parameter affine fit recovers $A=1.041$, $B=-2.75\,\mu $m, close to the zero-intercept unit-slope line the physics predicts. Source: \texttt {Array\_Design/array\_optimization/stage0\_ghost\_validation/} \texttt {ghost\_formula\_validation.png}, mtime 2026-08-14, the original validation run this pipeline's own \texttt {engine/ghost\_formula.py} was copied from (byte-for-byte verified against this same dataset, \texttt {VALIDATION.md}).}}{30}{figure.5.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Per-hole vignetting transmission $T_{\mathrm {hole}}(\varphi )=A_{\mathrm {overlap}}/(\pi R_h^2)$ vs.\ gaze angle $\varphi $, three hole diameters, $R_p=2000\,\mu $m, $R_{\mathrm {rot}}=13.5$\,mm. Transmission is exactly $1.0$ (full illumination) until the pupil begins to slide off the hole, then falls to exactly $0.0$ over a narrow angular transition -- the sharpness of this cutoff (a few degrees, not a gradual taper) is what makes vignetting a genuine on/off hazard rather than a smooth brightness gradient. The dashed vertical lines mark $|\varphi |=25^\circ $, this formula's (Equation~\ref {eq:pupil-shift}) own stated small-angle validity limit -- Chapter~\ref {ch:model-b}'s $27^\circ $ extrapolated bin sits just outside it, flagged there as extrapolated, not silently trusted. Source: \texttt {Array\_Design/array\_optimization/vignetting\_validation.png}, mtime 2026-08-15, the original validation run \texttt {vignetting\_overlap.py} was copied from.}}{33}{figure.5.2}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces Median worst-case ghost margin vs.\ $N$ (holes requested), all 11 geometry families, every $M$ and the global scope -- the full picture behind the winner table above. Square (cyan) and, separately, golden\_angle (red) are the only families that stay non-negative at $M=0$D and global scope; at every other $M$, square is again the only family that survives with a positive/infinite margin, while most of the remaining 9 families sit well below the $y=0$ safety line regardless of $N$ -- visual confirmation that Model A's structural-safety requirement is a genuinely narrow target, not an easy bar most designs clear. Source: \texttt {array\_data/evaluation\_results/family\_ranking\_margin\_vs\_N.png}, mtime 2026-09-13 00:37 (pre-generated by this project's own \texttt {family\_ranking} analysis, not produced for this paper).}}{42}{figure.6.1}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.1}{\ignorespaces All three states this section's gate distinguishes, shown side by side for the same two real candidates swept through the identical $(\varphi ,\theta )$ gaze path -- Case A: \texttt {archimedean\_spiral}, $M=4$D (a real, rejected candidate, $90.79\%$ time-weighted blind); Case B: the shipped hexagonal $M=8$D primary winner ($0.33\%$ blind). Left: Case~A \textbf {BLIND} ($\varphi =0.2^\circ ,\theta =82.0^\circ $, 0 holes reach the retina). Middle: Case~A \textbf {GHOST} (5 simultaneously active holes at $\varphi =24.5^\circ ,\theta =243.4^\circ $) -- note the images coincide near $\varphi =0$ but a real ghost-separation only appears at higher $\varphi $, consistent with $\Delta r(s,M)=sM/(P_0-M)$ vanishing only at $M=0$, not at $\varphi =0$. Right: both cases \textbf {clean}. Frames extracted via \texttt {ffmpeg} from \texttt {Array\_Design/array\_geometry\_lab/blindness\_mechanism\_animation/} \texttt {blindness\_mechanism\_animation.mp4} (mtime 2026-09-16 12:58); the underlying simulation reuses \texttt {engine/vignetting\_overlap.py}'s validated overlap-area formula and \texttt {engine/ghost\_formula.py}'s validated separation formula unmodified -- the same two functions used throughout Chapter~\ref {ch:array-theory}.}}{44}{figure.7.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.2}{\ignorespaces Real regenerated nearest-neighbor structure at identical spacing (pitch/ring-spacing $=4000\,\mu $m, $a_{\mathrm {hole}}=750\,\mu $m, $M=2$D). Square: two distinct nearest-neighbor distances, 4000 and $5657\,\mu $m ($=4000\sqrt 2$, confirming the diagonal weak direction exactly). Hexagonal: one distinct distance, all six neighbors equidistant. Ring designs (6 and 8 holes/ring): genuinely offset, non-uniform ring radii, not a simple lattice -- shown as real coordinate scatter rather than forced into the same distance-count framing. Source: this analysis, regenerated directly from \texttt {array\_calculation/degeneracy\_filter.py}'s own generator functions; see Chapter~\ref {ch:provenance}.}}{46}{figure.7.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.3}{\ignorespaces Real, already-computed gaze-weighted blind\%/ghost\% for the same four matched-spacing designs as Figure~\ref {fig:geom-structure}, read directly from \texttt {array\_data/theoretical\_data/gaze\_weighted\_metrics.csv} (not recomputed for this figure). Hexagonal is the only design with $0.00\%$ on both axes simultaneously; the ring-of-8 design trades blind\% for a real $7.58\%$ ghost fraction -- the discrete angular-gap tension (Section~\ref {sec:blinding}) hexagonal packing avoids by being simultaneously dense and well-separated in every direction.}}{46}{figure.7.3}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.1}{\ignorespaces Real hole layouts, Model~B (gaze-weighted, current primary standard) winning design per $M$ -- $M=0,1,2\text {--}3,4\text {--}5,6,8$D and the global (worst-case-across-$M$) scope. Source: \texttt {Array\_Design/array\_geometry\_lab/Design Visualisation/pngs\_for\_presentation/} \texttt {GazeWeighted\_*.png}, all 7 files mtime 2026-09-17 21:32 (verified via \texttt {stat}).}}{50}{figure.8.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.2}{\ignorespaces Real hole layouts, Model~A (single-axis worst-case gate, superseded primary standard, Section~\ref {sec:model-a-limitation}) winning design per $M$. Note the family mix (ring\_offset\_concentric dominates here, versus hexagonal's dominance in Figure~\ref {fig:layout-gaze-weighted}) -- the visible consequence of the methodology change documented in Chapter~\ref {ch:model-b}. Source: same folder as above, \texttt {WorstCase\_*.png}, all 7 files mtime 2026-09-17 21:32.}}{51}{figure.8.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.3}{\ignorespaces Real, simulated retinal light-intensity distribution (straight-ahead gaze) at $M=0,4,8$D, using each $M$'s own real \texttt {PRIMARY\_WINNER\_PER\_M} aperture (2000, 600, 400\,$\mu $m respectively). Computed directly from \texttt {stage2\_fixed}'s ASM propagation, $N_{\mathrm {grid}}=4096$, identical parameters to the rest of this paper; each panel normalized to its own peak intensity (absolute brightness is not radiometrically comparable across panels without a source-power calibration this project has not made; the blur \emph {shape} and relative \emph {size} growing from $M=0$ to $M=8$ are real). Source: this analysis, direct call to \texttt {stage2\_fixed.phase2\_retina.run\_pipeline\_point}; see Chapter~\ref {ch:provenance}.}}{52}{figure.8.3}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.1}{\ignorespaces The flatness prediction (Section~\ref {sec:flatness-prediction}): predicted LogMAR vs.\ myopia power at fixed aperture. Recomputed directly from the current \texttt {new\_sweeps/acuity\_sweep\_stage4.csv} (mtime 2026-09-13 00:11) for this paper; spread at $a=400\,\mu $m is $0.0166$ LogMAR across the full 8-dioptre range, against $1.687$ LogMAR at $a=2000\,\mu $m -- a $102\times $ difference.}}{54}{figure.9.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.2}{\ignorespaces Model deviation (predicted $-$ observed LogMAR) vs.\ aperture, post-fix, all three tested myopia powers. Deviation sign is not constant across the aperture range -- the model is measurably optimistic (predicts better vision than observed) at small apertures and pessimistic at some large-aperture/high-$M$ combinations, i.e.\ this is not a single universal bias. Source: this analysis, from \texttt {wang2009\_validation\_postfix\_predicted.csv}.}}{55}{figure.9.2}%
